id: mem-e1664d92
name: User reviews are asynchronous — don't block on them
description: >-
  When user can't do a full review in-session, don't pause work. Track the review as a task with a
  descriptive status, note the work done, and keep firing forward work.


  When a code/design review is requested and the user can't do it themselves in the moment, **do NOT
  pause forward progress waiting for them**. Instead:


  1. Create a Task with a clear subject (e.g., "Review v6.0.4 Wave 2 (de-enforcement gating)")

  2. Use the task `description` to capture WHAT was done — file paths, key risks, what to verify

  3. Task `status` reflects the review state (pending = awaiting user, in_progress = user is
  reviewing now, completed = user signed off)

  4. Keep firing the next forward step (don't gate it on the review)


  **Why:** User stated 2026-04-26 — "I can't do full reviews right now so let's keep moving forward
  but maybe we track them as tasks for me to review later. So add them as tasks and note the work
  done. And status is the review related one"


  **How to apply:** When the always-team memory ("never bypass orchestration") would suggest a
  reviewer pass at a checkpoint, AND the user hasn't time to engage with the review, swap "fire
  reviewer agent now" for "create review task + keep building." Critical-correctness work (gates,
  security, auth) still warrants a reviewer-agent pass; THIS rule is about the user's personal
  review cadence, not skipping reviewer-agent automation.
category: documentation
trigger: postflight
severity: advisory
check:
  type: context-checked
  params: {}
enabled: false
